{"title":"Elevation, slope, and wind speed","markdown":{"headingText":"Elevation, slope, and wind speed","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-e05","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> **Dates of practical:** 6th and 13th November 2025\n\nDigital Elevation Models (DEMs) describe land surface height in a grid and are a special case of continuous geospatial data, widely available as raster datasets at various spatial resolutions. GIS provides tools to handle DEMs.\n\nYou will find data for this exercise on the **L:** drive in the usual place. Read alongside **GIS_guide_to_practical_exercises**.\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n## Learning aims\n\nVisualise DEMs and wind speed data; subset, classify and vectorize; create slope and aspect images. These steps are vital for the wind farm Project.\n:::\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Investigating the topography of Wales\n\n(@) Open the raster layer `SRTM_Wales_DEM_25m.tif` (from `Wales_DSM`). This DEM is derived from Space Shuttle data (2000) at 25 m resolution.\n\n(@) In **Layer Properties**, experiment with **Singleband pseudocolor**. Adjust colour ramps, Min/Max, and mode/classes.\n\n(@) Under **Color ramp**, **Create new color ramp → cpt‑city**. Explore topography styles and experiment with Min/Max, mode (continuous/equal interval), and classes.\n\n(@) Install the **Value Tool** plugin and enable it (View → Panels → Value Tool Panel). Use it to interrogate raster values and find the heights of Snowdon and Pen y Fan. Validate heights using external sources.\n\n(@) Open `Wales_operational_wind farms` and observe that wind farms are often on coasts or high ground.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Examining the topography of your map tile\n\n(@) Subset the DEM to your map tile via **Raster → Extraction → Clip Raster by Mask Layer**. Input: Wales DEM; Mask layer: your map tile. Save with a sensible name on OneDrive.\n\n(@) Explore your subset with suitable palettes and ranges. Identify the highest point and, if possible, its name.\n\n(@) Open **View → New 3D Map View**. Configure: Terrain type = **DEM (Raster Layer)**; Elevation = your clipped DEM; Vertical scale = 4; Tile resolution = 64 px. Explore in 3D and experiment with settings. Add other layers if useful.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Slope, aspect and hillshade\n\n(@) Create a slope image (**Raster → Analysis → Slope**). Ensure slope is in **degrees**. Identify areas of highest slope and explain why.\n\n(@) Create an aspect image (**Raster → Analysis → Aspect**). Because 1° and 359° are near‑equivalents, choose a cyclic CPT (e.g., wave from cpt‑city → QGIS).\n\n(@) Create a hillshade (**Raster → Analysis → Hillshade**). Combine with your DEM by placing hillshade above the DEM with ~50% opacity and a grayscale to enhance relief.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Classifying raster data\n\n(@) Open **Raster Calculator** and classify slopes > 6° as unsuitable. For example, if your slope layer is `SS68_slope.tif`, use an expression like `\"SS68_slope@1\" > 6` with **Select Layer Extent** and output `SS68_slope_classified`. Pixels with value 1 are unsuitable (slope > 6°), 0 otherwise.\n\n(@) Adjust transparency of the classified slope image and inspect unsuitable areas.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Converting to vector polygons\n\n(@) Vectorise the classified raster (**Raster → Conversion → Polygonize**). Input: your classified slope layer (slope < 6° suitable).\n\n(@) Under **Layer Properties**, set **Categorized** symbology on field `DN` to distinguish suitable (0) and unsuitable (1) polygons.\n\n(@) Use **Select by Expression** (DN = 1) to select unsuitable polygons and **Export → Save Selected Features As** to create a constraint layer.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Wind speeds\n\n(@) Open raster `Wales_wind-speed_50m.tif` from `Wales_mean_windspeed` and choose an appropriate palette (see the `description_and_data_source.txt` file for details).\n\n(@) Clip the wind speed raster to your tile and visualise with a suitable palette.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Putting it all together\n\n(@) Reopen your constraint layers from Exercise 4 alongside your polygonised slope constraint. With the wind speed layer beneath constraints, explore where development may be permitted and optimal (high wind, large areas).\n\n(@) Design **Map 3** of your wind farm Project to clearly show the best otherwise‑unconstrained wind farm location and justify why.\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n# Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this exercise you should:\n\n1. Visualise, clip, and extract values from raster data.\n2. Create DEM products (slope, aspect, hillshade).\n3. Classify a raster layer and create polygons from the classification.\n4. Recognise wind speed variation and identify the best wind farm location.\n:::\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwind